      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment carefully.\n\nThis is part 3 of 4 of an OIML certificate R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 7 (water meters, R49 is water meters for cold potable water).\n\nThe fragment contains:\n- Certificate metadata (appears on each page): Number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 7, Project number 3528374, pages 8-11 of 12, Member State: The Netherlands\n- Table 5: General characteristics of the family of instruments — Full bore - Battery / Renewable energy powered. Matrix of meter size × accuracy class → Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4 flow rates and Q3/Q1 ratio.\n- Continuation table on page 9 (same table, more sizes).\n- Note about flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4 freely chosen (footnote-like conditions).\n- Table 6: Indicating device, Reduced bore – AC mains powered\n- Table 7: Indicating device, Reduced bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered\n- Table 8: Indicating device, Full bore – AC mains powered\n- Table 9 heading appears (Full bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered) but its content is not in this fragment (it likely appears in part 4).\n\nSo I need to produce a JSON with:\n- certificate: number, scheme (A, since \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\"), project_number, page_total (12), member_state (The Netherlands). Date issued not present in this fragment. oiml_issuer_id — not present.\n- issuing_authority: NMi — name \"NMi\" (this fragment only shows \"NMi\" header). Address not present. Maybe just name.\n- model_family: many DN sizes with accuracy class variants.\n- matrix_tables: Table 5 (flow characteristics full bore battery/renewable), Table 6, 7, 8 (indicating device characteristics).\n- characteristics: model_level for q1, q2, q3, q4, q3/q1 ratio.\n- footnotes: the note about flow rates freely chosen could be captured as footnotes or as description.\n\nLet me think about the model structure. The models here are combinations of meter size (DN40..DN1800) and accuracy class (1 or 2). For Table 5 (full bore, battery/renewable), sizes range DN40 to DN1650, with some sizes having two rows (class 1 and class 2) and others having \"1 & 2\" combined.\n\nModel ID could be like \"DN40 class 1\", \"DN40 class 2\", \"DN65 class 1&2\", etc.\n\nSince this is a fragment of a larger cert, other parts probably contain the reduced bore table (Tables 1-4 presumably) and other info. But instruction says: extract only content present in THIS fragment. So I should focus on tables 5-8 and the notes.\n\nThe model_level characteristics: For each model (meter size + accuracy class), q1, q2, q3, q4 values in m³/h, and q3_q1_ratio (dimensionless).\n\nLet me enumerate Table 5 rows (full bore, battery/renewable):\n\nPage 8:\n- DN40, class 1: Q1=0.32, Q2=0.512, Q3=40, Q4=50, Q3/Q1=125\n- DN40, class 2: Q1=0.16, Q2=0.256, Q3=40, Q4=50, Q3/Q1=250\n- DN50, class 1: Q1=0.504, Q2=0.8064, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio 125\n- DN50, class 2: Q1=0.252, Q2=0.4032, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, ratio 250\n- DN65, class 1 & 2: Q1=0.4, Q2=0.64, Q3=100, Q4=125, ratio 250\n- DN80, class 1 & 2: Q1=0.64, Q2=1.024, Q3=160, Q4=200, ratio 250\n- DN100, class 1 & 2: Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 250\n- DN125, class 1 & 2: Q1=1, Q2=1.6, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, ratio 250\n- DN150, class 1: Q1=5.04, Q2=8.064, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 125\n- DN150, class 2: Q1=2.52, Q2=4.032, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, ratio 250\n- DN200, class 1: Q1=8, Q2=12.8, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio 125\n- DN200, class 2: Q1=4, Q2=6.4, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, ratio 250\n- DN250, class 1: Q1=12.8, Q2=20.48, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio 125\n- DN250, class 2: Q1=6.4, Q2=10.24, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, ratio 250\n- DN300, class 1: Q1=20, Q2=32, Q3=2500, Q4=3125, ratio 125\n- DN300, class 2: Q1=10, Q2=16, Q3=2500, Q4=3125, ratio 250\n- DN350, class 1 & 2: Q1=16, Q2=25.6, Q3=4000, Q4=5000, ratio 250\n- DN400, class 1 & 2: Q1=16, Q2=25.6, Q3=4000, Q4=5000, ratio 250\n- DN450, class 1 & 2: Q1=25.2, Q2=40.32, Q3=6300, Q4=7875, ratio 250\n- DN500, class 1 & 2: Q1=25.2, Q2=40.32, Q3=6300, Q4=7875, ratio 250\n- DN600, class 1: Q1=78.75, Q2=126, Q3=6300, Q4=7875, ratio 80\n- DN600, class 2: Q1=39.375, Q2=63, Q3=6300, Q4=7875, ratio 160\n- DN700, class 1: Q1=200, Q2=320, Q3=10000, Q4=12500, ratio 50\n- DN700, class 2: Q1=100, Q2=160, Q3=10000, Q4=12500, ratio 100\n- DN750, class 1: Q1=200, Q2=320, Q3=10000, Q4=12500, ratio 50\n- DN750, class 2: Q1=100, Q2=160, Q3=10000, Q4=12500, ratio 100\n\nPage 9 continuation:\n- DN800, class 1: Q1=200, Q2=320, Q3=10000, Q4=12500, ratio 50\n- DN800, class 2: Q1=100, Q2=160, Q3=10000, Q4=12500, ratio 100\n- DN900, class 2: Q1=158.7, Q2=254, Q3=10000, Q4=12500, ratio 63\n- DN1000, class 2: Q1=160, Q2=250, Q3=16000, Q4=20000, ratio 100\n- DN1050, class 2: Q1=160, Q2=250, Q3=16000, Q4=20000, ratio 100\n- DN1100, class 2: Q1=254, Q2=406.3, Q3=16000, Q4=20000, ratio 63\n- DN1200, class 2: Q1=254, Q2=406.3, Q3=16000, Q4=20000, ratio 63\n- DN1350, class 2: Q1=400, Q2=640, Q3=16000, Q4=20000, ratio 40\n- DN1400, class 2: Q1=400, Q2=640, Q3=16000, Q4=20000, ratio 40\n- DN1500, class 2: Q1=400, Q2=640, Q3=16000, Q4=20000, ratio 40\n- DN1600, class 2: Q1=400, Q2=640, Q3=16000, Q4=20000, ratio 40\n- DN1650, class 2: Q1=400, Q2=640, Q3=16000, Q4=20000, ratio 40\n\nThat's 26 models from Table 5 (page 8 has 26 rows... let me count: DN40×2, DN50×2, DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150×2, DN200×2, DN250×2, DN300×2, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600×2, DN700×2, DN750×2 = 2+2+1+1+1+1+2+2+2+2+1+1+1+1+2+2+2 = 26). Page 9 has 12 more rows = 38 models total in this table.\n\nNote DN1800 appears in Table 8 (indicating device) but not in Table 5 — interesting, full bore DN1800 has indicating device characteristics. The table 8 mentions DN1800. But flow rate table only goes to DN1650. I'll just capture as-is.\n\nNow, how to structure models? Each model is a combination of meter size and accuracy class. Model IDs like \"DN40_class1\", \"DN40_class2\", \"DN65_class1_2\"? Hmm. Let me use model_id like \"DN40 class 1\", \"DN40 class 2\", \"DN65 class 1 & 2\".\n\nActually, for the combined \"1 & 2\" rows, the same values apply to both classes. I could model as \"DN65 (class 1 & 2)\".\n\nFor model_level characteristics, attributes: q1, q2, q3, q4 (unit m³/h), q3_q1_ratio (dimensionless). Also accuracy_class per model.\n\nBut wait — model_level values must be structured values per the strict rules? The schema example in rules shows:\n\n```json\n{\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}, {\"model\": \"G2.5\", \"value\": 4.0}]}\n```\n\nSo value can be plain numbers in the model_level example (the rule 2 strict structure applies to characteristics generally, but the R137 example shows plain values). Hmm, there's tension. Rule 2 says \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape\". But the R137 example shows \"value\": 2.5 directly. I'll follow the R137 example for model_level since it's explicit, but maybe include unit_symbol at attribute level as shown. Actually the example in rule 4 shows unit_symbol at the attribute level of model_level entries: `{\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [...]}`. I'll follow that exact pattern.\n\nHmm, but rule 2 strict... The safest is to mimic the rule-4 R137 example format exactly, since it's given for this exact type of table (Q1/Q2/Q3/Q4 for water meters R49 is analogous). I'll use plain numeric values with unit_symbol at attribute level.\n\nAlso include footnote/conditions: The note after the tables says flow rates can be freely chosen subject to conditions. That's more of a general note; I can put it in matrix_tables description/footnotes or in footnotes[]. Let me add it to footnotes as several entries or as a description. Since it doesn't have numbered markers, maybe put it in the matrix_table description.\n\nNow the indicating device tables (6, 7, 8). Each maps meter sizes (groups) to:\n- Indicating range (maximum value) [m³]\n- Indicating range (minimum value) [m³]\n- Verification scale interval (maximum value) [m³]\n\nThese are config_level characteristics: the axis is power supply type (AC mains vs Battery/Renewable) and bore (reduced vs full). Actually the tables are per configuration: bore × power supply.\n\nTable 6: Reduced bore – AC mains powered:\n- DN40, DN50, DN65: max=9999999, min=99999, VSI=0.0001\n- DN80: max=9999999, min=999999, VSI=0.0001\n- DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200: max=9999999, min=999999, VSI=0.001\n- DN250: max=9999999, min=9999999, VSI=0.001\n- DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: max=9999999, min=9999999, VSI=0.01\n\nTable 7: Reduced bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered:\n- DN40, DN50: max=9999999, min=99999, VSI=0.0001\n- DN65: max=9999999, min=99999, VSI=0.001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: max=9999999, min=999999, VSI=0.001\n- DN200: max=9999999, min=999999, VSI=0.01\n- DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450: max=9999999, min=9999999, VSI=0.01\n- DN500, DN600: max=9999999, min=9999999, VSI=0.1\n\nTable 8: Full bore – AC mains powered:\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001\n- DN200, DN250, DN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01\n- DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1\n- DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1\n- DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800: 999999999, 99999999, 1\n\nTable 9 heading exists but no content — I should not fabricate. Just note that Table 9 has no content in this fragment; omit.\n\nHow to represent these indicating device tables? Options:\n1. As matrix_tables with rows per meter-size group.\n2. As config_level characteristics with axis \"power supply / bore\".\n\nI think matrix_tables is most faithful. And maybe also config_level entries for verification_scale_interval etc. But that could get very verbose. Given the fragment covers 3 tables with grouped rows, I'll emit them as matrix_tables with column names like meter_size_group, indicating_range_max_m3, indicating_range_min_m3, verification_scale_interval_max_m3.\n\nValues must be numbers with decimal points fixed (0,0001 → 0.0001; 0,01 → 0.01; 0,1 → 0.1).\n\nLet me decide the JSON structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"revision\": \"7\",  // schema doesn't have revision in certificate... hmm\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"3528374\",\n    \"page_total\": 12,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n  },\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nWait, certificate schema fields: number, scheme (A|B), project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. There's no revision field in certificate. But the number is \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 7\". Hmm. The revision_history[] exists in the schema, but that's for revision history entries with dates/changes — not present here. I could include the revision in number? \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" with revision noted... The schema doesn't have a revision field for the certificate. Rule 8: don't fabricate. I could put \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and maybe add revision info... The safest is number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and omit revision, or include \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 7\"? Hmm. Since this is a fragment and other parts likely capture the certificate header, I'll include number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and maybe add a \"revision\": \"7\"? Adding extra fields is risky but the schema says \"layered schema\" — it's a guide. I think adding \"revision\" under certificate is reasonable given the layered schema's revision_history uses \"revision\" strings. Hmm, but strictly following schema, extra keys may break validation. Let me be conservative: certificate.number = \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\", and I could note revision in... Actually many extraction tasks tolerate extra fields. The instruction says \"produce a JSON object following the layered schema\". Extra key \"revision\" seems harmless and informative. But strictly, \"If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it\" — revision IS present (\"revision 7\"). I'll include \"revision\": \"7\" in certificate. That seems within the spirit.\n\nActually wait — revision_history is a separate array for \"revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0), date, changes\". There's no date/changes info here, so revision_history entries can't be fabricated. I'll just put revision in certificate.\n\nscheme: The number contains \"-A-\" indicating scheme A. I'll include scheme: \"A\".\n\nissuing_authority: name \"NMi\". Only the name appears in the fragment header. Include name only. Maybe oiml_issuer_id not present. NMi Certificates... no ID given here. So issuing_authority: {name: \"NMi\"}.\n\ncertified_type: category — R49 is \"Water meter\" — but is that stated in this fragment? The fragment doesn't say \"water meter\" explicitly. Tables mention \"Meter size\", \"family of instruments\", \"indicating device\". Category could be \"Water meter\" from R49 knowledge... Rule 8: don't fabricate. The fragment doesn't state it. Hmm. But \"Meter\" is clearly indicated. R49 = water meters. I think it's safe to say category \"Water meter\" — but strictly it's inference. The instruction says extract from the markdown. Other parts of the cert would state it. Since this is a fragment and we should only include what's present... I could set category: \"Water meter\" as it's strongly implied by \"Meter size\" and R49. I'll include it — it's a reasonable canonical categorization, and R49 is the water meter recommendation. Actually, let me include type_designations? Not present in fragment. module_designation: null? The indicating device is a module... The tables refer to \"the indicating device\" as a component. I could add components[] with role \"indicating device\". Hmm, the indicating device characteristics are described per configuration — these are components of the meter.\n\nLet me think: should the indicating device tables be components[] entries with characteristics? The schema components[]: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. The characteristics structure inside components isn't fully defined. Probably cleaner: matrix_tables for tables 6-8, since they're multi-dim tables (meter size group × 3 attributes × 4 configurations... actually each table is one configuration).\n\nI'll do:\n- matrix_tables: 4 entries:\n  1. \"Table 5 — General characteristics of the family of instruments (Full bore, Battery / Renewable energy powered)\": columns meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio; rows for all 38 combos.\n  2. \"Table 6 — Indicating device (Reduced bore, AC mains powered)\"\n  3. \"Table 7 — Indicating device (Reduced bore, Battery / Renewable energy powered)\"\n  4. \"Table 8 — Indicating device (Full bore, AC mains powered)\"\n\n- Also populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level for the flow rates (per rule 4 R137 example). With 38 models that's a lot but correct.\n\nModel naming: \"DN40 class 1\", \"DN40 class 2\", \"DN65 class 1 & 2\"... Hmm, using \"1 & 2\" in model id is awkward. Alternative: expand combined rows into separate models? No — don't fabricate; the cert lists them combined, but the values apply to both classes. I think keeping \"DN65 class 1 & 2\" as a single model entry is faithful. Actually for the model_level characteristics, I'd have entries like:\n\n{\"attribute\": \"q1\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"DN40 class 1\", \"value\": 0.32}, {\"model\": \"DN40 class 2\", \"value\": 0.16}, {\"model\": \"DN65 class 1 & 2\", \"value\": 0.4}, ...]}\n\nThat's 38 values per attribute × 5 attributes (q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio) — long but fine.\n\nHmm, but wait — this is a fragment of a 4-part cert. Part 1/2 probably contain the reduced bore flow table (Tables 1-4) and other details. The instruction: \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only.\" So include only what's here.\n\nGiven the full cert covers both reduced and full bore, and both AC and battery power, the \"model family\" really is meter size × accuracy class × bore × power supply. But from this fragment, the flow table is only full bore battery/renewable. So models derived here are those. However, indicating tables cover other configs. To be faithful and avoid double counting, I'll present model_family.models for the flow-rate table models (full bore, battery/renewable powered) — that's what rule 4 asks. And also accuracy_class per model.\n\nShould I add \"accuracy_class\" as a model_level characteristic? The schema says model_level entries have attribute, unit, values. accuracy_class: values 1, 2, \"1 & 2\". I'll include it as a model_level attribute with unit null.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider: maybe encode model_id as \"DN40 class 1\" etc. and include accuracy_class attribute. That's redundant but explicit. I'll do it for clarity? The R137 example includes size in the model_id and doesn't repeat it as attribute. I'll skip accuracy_class as separate attribute since it's embedded in model_id... Actually, to be safe and useful for downstream, I'll include it. Hmm, it makes it consistent: model_id \"DN40 class 1\", attribute \"accuracy_class\" value 1. Fine, include.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the model_id format. Options: \"DN40/1\" or \"DN40 class 1\" or \"DN40-1\". I'll use \"DN40 class 1\". And for combined: \"DN65 class 1 & 2\". \n\nWait — for the combined rows, does the flow data differ by class? No — same Q values, different ratio (250 for both classes in one cell? Actually \"1 & 2\" rows have single ratio value, e.g., DN65 ratio 250 for both classes). Interesting: for DN65 both classes have ratio 250. For DN40 class1 ratio 125, class2 250. OK.\n\nNow config_level: The bore (reduced/full) and power supply (AC/battery) are configuration axes. The flow-rate table here is only one config (full bore battery/renewable). Should I mark these as config_level? The models themselves (DN sizes + class) span configs... For simplicity, per rule 5, config axes could capture indicating-device attributes across 4 configs. Let me think about whether to also emit config_level entries for verification scale interval etc.\n\nI think a clean approach:\n- matrix_tables: preserve all 4 tables faithfully.\n- characteristics.model_level: flow rates q1-q4 + ratio per model (full bore battery/renewable) — per rule 4 mandate.\n- characteristics.config_level: indicating device attributes (indicating_range_max, indicating_range_min, verification_scale_interval_max) across the axis \"bore_and_power_supply\" with conditions like \"reduced_bore_ac_mains\", \"reduced_bore_battery_renewable\", \"full_bore_ac_mains\". Each value would be... but values vary per meter size within a config! E.g., reduced bore AC mains: VSI is 0.0001 for DN40-65, 0.001 for DN100-250, 0.01 for DN300-600. So a single config-level value per condition doesn't work — it varies by size. So config_level values would need condition strings that include the size groups. That gets ugly: condition \"reduced bore, AC mains, DN40–DN65\". Hmm.\n\nThe rule 5 says config_level for \"attribute × config-axis matrix\" with values {condition, value}. The condition could be \"DN40-DN65 (reduced bore, AC mains)\". That's stretching it. Alternatively, just keep indicating tables in matrix_tables only, which is exactly what matrix_tables is for (\"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\"). Yes — indicating device tables fit matrix_tables perfectly. I'll put them there and skip config_level to avoid noise. Actually, maybe include a few config_level entries for verification_scale_interval with compound conditions? I'll skip — matrix_tables preserves it well, and over-nesting creates fabrication risk.\n\nHmm, but wait — should I also include the \"indicating device\" as a component? components[]: \"include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies\". The cert names \"the indicating device\" as part of the family. I could add:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"role\": \"indicating device\",\n  \"type_designations\": [],\n  \"alternatives\": null,\n  \"characteristics\": {}\n}\n```\n\nThat's empty-ish. Probably better to omit components since no type designations are named for it. The tables describe its characteristics per configuration. I'll omit components.\n\nFootnotes: The paragraph after Table 5 (page 9):\n\n\"Please note that the flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3 and Q4 can be freely chosen as long as:\n- Values Q3 and ratio Q3/Q1 are selected from paragraph 4.1 of OIML R49-1:2013.\n- Values mentioned for Q1 and Q2 are minimum values and the ratio Q2/Q1 = 1,6.\n- Values mentioned for Q3 and Q4 are maximum values and the ratio Q4/Q3 = 1,25.\n- The ratio Q3/Q1 is at least 40.\"\n\nThese aren't numbered footnotes; they're general conditions. I could capture them in the matrix_table's \"description\" for Table 5, and/or as footnotes with a marker like \"note\". Footnotes schema: marker, text. I could add footnotes entries with markers \"note-1\".. hmm, fabricating markers. Better: put the note text in the matrix_tables description of the flow table. And maybe also include the note as description text. Let me put it into the Table 5 matrix_table.description.\n\nAlso, these conditions mean Q1/Q2 are minimums and Q3/Q4 are maximums — relevant for interpreting values. The description can carry that.\n\nNow decimal conversion: 0,32 → 0.32; 0,512 → 0.512; 78,75 → 78.75; 312,5 → 312.5; 40,32 → 40.32; 25,6 → 25.6; 12,8 → 12.8; 20,48 → 20.48; 10,24 → 10.24; 0,8064 → 0.8064; 0,4032 → 0.4032; 1,024 → 1.024; 0,256 → 0.256; 0,64 → 0.64; 6,4 → 6.4; 39,375 → 39.375; 158,7 → 158.7; 406,3 → 406.3; 0,0001 → 0.0001; 0,001 → 0.001; 0,01 → 0.01; 0,1 → 0.1.\n\nNote JSON numbers: 0.0001 fine.\n\nNow build the full Table 5 rows list (name: \"General characteristics of the family of instruments — Full bore, Battery / Renewable energy powered\"):\n\nColumns: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio. Units: m³/h for q1-q4; ratio dimensionless. column_units: [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null].\n\nRows (38):\n1. DN40, \"1\", 0.32, 0.512, 40, 50, 125\n2. DN40, \"2\", 0.16, 0.256, 40, 50, 250\n3. DN50, \"1\", 0.504, 0.8064, 63, 78.75, 125\n4. DN50, \"2\", 0.252, 0.4032, 63, 78.75, 250\n5. DN65, \"1 & 2\", 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250\n6. DN80, \"1 & 2\", 0.64, 1.024, 160, 200, 250\n7. DN100, \"1 & 2\", 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n8. DN125, \"1 & 2\", 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\n9. DN150, \"1\", 5.04, 8.064, 630, 787.5, 125\n10. DN150, \"2\", 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\n11. DN200, \"1\", 8, 12.8, 1000, 1250, 125\n12. DN200, \"2\", 4, 6.4, 1000, 1250, 250\n13. DN250, \"1\", 12.8, 20.48, 1600, 2000, 125\n14. DN250, \"2\", 6.4, 10.24, 1600, 2000, 250\n15. DN300, \"1\", 20, 32, 2500, 3125, 125\n16. DN300, \"2\", 10, 16, 2500, 3125, 250\n17. DN350, \"1 & 2\", 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n18. DN400, \"1 & 2\", 16, 25.6, 4000, 5000, 250\n19. DN450, \"1 & 2\", 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n20. DN500, \"1 & 2\", 25.2, 40.32, 6300, 7875, 250\n21. DN600, \"1\", 78.75, 126, 6300, 7875, 80\n22. DN600, \"2\", 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\n23. DN700, \"1\", 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n24. DN700, \"2\", 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n25. DN750, \"1\", 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n26. DN750, \"2\", 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n27. DN800, \"1\", 200, 320, 10000, 12500, 50\n28. DN800, \"2\", 100, 160, 10000, 12500, 100\n29. DN900, \"2\", 158.7, 254, 10000, 12500, 63\n30. DN1000, \"2\", 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100\n31. DN1050, \"2\", 160, 250, 16000, 20000, 100\n32. DN1100, \"2\", 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63\n33. DN1200, \"2\", 254, 406.3, 16000, 20000, 63\n34. DN1350, \"2\", 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n35. DN1400, \"2\", 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n36. DN1500, \"2\", 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n37. DN1600, \"2\", 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n38. DN1650, \"2\", 400, 640, 16000, 20000, 40\n\nAccuracy class: keep as string \"1\", \"2\", \"1 & 2\"? Or numbers for 1/2? String \"1\" might get parsed as number 1. Use number